Veymelo
Copyright 2026 hassandaoh

Much of what Veymelo can do was learned by reading EffectCraft
(https://github.com/storytold/effectcraft), MIT OR Apache-2.0. The code here is written
for Veymelo; where something was taken over from EffectCraft it is listed below.

- crates/keyframe/src/lib.rs, test `overlapping_influences_match_measured_values`: the
  sample values (Bezier eases whose influences add up to more than 100 %, measured in a
  compositing application) come from EffectCraft's keyframe tests.
- crates/color/src/blend.rs: which blend modes keep colours brighter than white, and the
  classic modes fading their colour toward a neutral one by opacity, follow what EffectCraft
  found (and measured) in a compositing application.
- crates/expr: how loopOut and loopIn find the stretch that repeats and work out pingpong,
  offset and continue; wiggle's octaves each starting at a phase of their own; gaussRandom's
  spread (nine in ten within 0 to 1); the small allowance in timeToFrames and posterizeTime;
  and the ease, easeIn and easeOut curves follow EffectCraft's expression prelude.
- crates/effects/src/puppet: the way the puppet is built follows EffectCraft's — a mesh over
  the layer's alpha (thresholded at a half, grown by a distance transform, the region under a
  seed with its holes filled, traced along cell edges, simplified, densified, a jittered
  hexagonal lattice inside, Delaunay triangles with the outline's edges recovered by flips),
  the two-step as-rigid-as-possible solve with conjugate gradients, bend and advanced pins as
  side targets about the pin (weight 10), starch as triangle stiffness, overlap as triangle
  depth, and drawing each pixel from the front-most triangle with four-sample coverage; also
  the defaults (350 triangles, density 50, expansion 3, refinement 20).
- crates/effects/src/blur.rs: the blur family follows EffectCraft's — blurriness as about twice
  the Gaussian deviation, Repeat Edge Pixels, how Channel Blur divides each blurred channel by
  its alpha blurred alike, Compound Blur's blends between blur levels by the map, the bilateral
  and smart blurs, Cross Blur's transfer modes, Radial Fast Blur's streak length and its
  brightest and darkest modes, Vector Blur's types, and Camera Lens Blur's iris rows, highlight
  gain and saturation, diffraction fringe (rim weights) and blur map.
- crates/effects/src/color.rs, grade.rs, look.rs: the colour correction family follows
  EffectCraft's — notably Brightness & Contrast's modern curve (its constants were fitted by
  EffectCraft to a compositing tool's output), Hue/Saturation's ranges and fall-offs, Levels'
  clipping choices, Black & White's grey from the smallest channel and the colourfulness,
  Colorama's phases, palettes and matching, Shadow/Highlight's area brightness and amounts,
  Selective Color's range weights, Broadcast Colors' limits, the automatic corrections'
  histograms and clips, and the colour grade's steps and made-up looks.
- crates/effects/src/generate.rs, paint.rs, forms.rs: the generate and paint family follows
  EffectCraft's — the twenty blend choices of the patterns, Cell Pattern's cell kinds, the
  light rays, burst and sweep, Scribble's wiggle and Vegas' segments, Write-on's brush timing,
  Paint Bucket's tolerance and spread, Radio Waves (waves born at the frequency, growing for
  their lifespan, their parameters read at birth, contours as signed distances, the stroke
  profiles and the soft edge of 0.5 + 1.5 / quality pixels), Advanced Lightning (midpoint
  displacement of 0.22 of a piece's length times turbulence, the lightning types, the alpha
  obstacle, decay, core drain and fork strength), and Fractal's sets, palettes and edge
  oversampling.
- crates/effects/src/distort.rs, warp.rs, bends.rs, liquid.rs: the distort family follows
  EffectCraft's — notably Twirl's and Ripple's radii as parts of the layer, Bulge's taper and
  pinned edges, Wave Warp's wave shapes and pinning, Transform's shutter, Turbulent Displace's
  kinds and pinning (locked ones easing over a quarter of the layer), Displacement Map's
  sources and behaviours, Optics Compensation's resize limits and optimal pixels, Magnify's
  links and scalings, Bezier Warp's patch, the bend, lens, page-turn, tiler and griddler
  geometry, Bender's styles, Blobbylize's lighting, Power Pin's expansion and blend of even and
  perspective stretching, Slant, Smudge and Split, Smear's and Reshape's elasticity steps and
  correspondence points, the fifteen warp styles, the upscale's detail, Flow Motion's knots, and
  Liquify's brushes (dabs every quarter brush, the grid's size, the stroke text).
- crates/effects/src/stylize.rs, crafts.rs: the stylize family follows EffectCraft's — Glow's A & B
  looping and midpoint, its operations and arbitrary map, Mosaic, Find Edges' and Emboss's
  kernels, Cartoon's steps (smoothing guided by brightness, the shading steps and the edge
  black level and contrast), Roughen Edges' types, Strobe Light's operators, Texturize's relief,
  Brush Strokes' cells, Kaleidoscope's wedges, the tilings, the vignette's falloff, the
  thresholds, Block Load's levels, Burn Film's charred rim, Glass and Plastic's lighting, the
  hexagonal tiles and Smoothie's palette.
- crates/effects/src/grain.rs, film.rs: the noise and grain family follows EffectCraft's — Fractal
  Noise's engine (value noise on a lattice in x, y and the evolution, the four noise types with
  soft linear as a quadratic B-spline and its gain of (181/231)/0.55, the fractal types' shapes and
  dynamic warps, sub settings, perspective offset, turbulence factor and overflows), Curl Noise,
  Noise Alpha's and Noise HLS's kinds, the grain controls (presets, tones about a midpoint, the
  blending modes), grain measured in the flattest boxes (spread about a softened self, size from
  neighbours' likeness), Remove Grain's guided smoothing, texture and temporal pass, and the
  median found by sliding a count of values (Huang's method).
- crates/effects/src/channel.rs: the channel family follows EffectCraft's — Invert's colour spaces,
  the channel sources, Channel Combiner's conversions and where a single value goes, Minimax's
  channels and edges, Arithmetic's and Compound Arithmetic's operators and overflow, Solid
  Composite, Remove Color Matting, Set Matte's premultiplied and composited matte, Blend's modes,
  Calculations' channels and Composite's ways of bringing the original back.
- crates/effects/src/matte.rs, keys.rs, screen.rs: the matte and keying families follow
  EffectCraft's — Matte Choker's stages, the refiners' edge band, smoothing, chatter, motion blur
  and decontamination, the colour keys' distances, Color Difference Key's partial mattes and
  levels, Color Range's spaces, Extract, Difference Matte, Inner/Outer Key's trimap, colour
  estimate and clean-up brushes, Unmult's ramp, the spill suppressors, Key Cleaner, Wire
  Removal's styles, and the screen keyer's method (biases, screen difference, clip and rollback,
  despot, masks, replace methods, colour suppression and balance, source crops).
- crates/effects/src/perspective.rs: the perspective family follows EffectCraft's — Drop Shadow,
  Radial Shadow's projection and glass edge, the bevels' lighting, the cylinder's and sphere's
  wrapping, turning and shading, Spotlight's cone, Environment's bumps and reflection mappings, and
  3D Glasses' views.
- crates/effects/src/transition.rs: the transition family follows EffectCraft's — Block Dissolve's
  blocks, the gradient and image wipes' soft bands, Iris Wipe's polygon and star, the linear,
  radial and venetian wipes, Glass Wipe's refraction, Grid Wipe's shapes and spread, Jaws' teeth,
  Light Wipe's band and ramp, Line Sweep's staggered strips, the scale wipes, Twister's ribbon and
  the warping reveal's reactors.
- crates/effects/src/time.rs: the time family follows EffectCraft's — Echo's operators, Posterize
  Time's held frames, Time Difference's alpha channel choices, Time Displacement's map and time
  resolution, Timewarp's settings (methods, speed or source frame, tuning, motion blur, matte and
  warp layers, show, crops), Force Motion Blur and Wide Time. The frames built between
  (per-pixel moves, what is hidden and uncovered) are our own.
- crates/effects/src/words.rs: Numbers and Timecode follow EffectCraft's — Numbers' types (leading
  zeros to five places, the date formats from 1 January 2000, hexadecimal), random values, the
  display options, and Timecode's formats and time sources. They are drawn in real fonts by our
  own text engine rather than a stroke font.
- crates/effects/src/utility.rs: the utility family follows EffectCraft's — Grow Bounds,
  Overbrights' channels and colours, HDR Highlight Compression's soft knee, HDR Compander's gain
  and gamma, and Cineon Converter's Kodak log conversion with its rolloff.
- crates/effects/src/colorfile.rs, ocio.rs, ocio/: the colour-management effects follow
  EffectCraft's — the OCIO effects' settings, the ASC CDL maths, the .3dl/.csp/.spi1d/.spi3d/
  .spimtx readers' conventions, the parts of OCIO configurations read, Color Profile Converter's
  profiles and intents, and Color Stabilizer's three ways. The colour spaces and curves
  (crates/color/src/space.rs) are from the published standards and camera makers' papers; the
  Common LUT Format reader, the YAML reader and the gamut limits drawn from a conversion are ours.
- crates/effects/src/weather.rs, particles.rs, hair.rs, playground.rs, cards.rs, shatter.rs,
  breakup.rs, water.rs, foam.rs: the simulation family follows EffectCraft's — each effect's
  settings, defaults and units, and how each behaves: the falling and rising things' depths and
  drift, the particle systems' producers, physics, kinds and looks, Hair's strands, Particle
  Playground's cannon, grid, exploders, forces and map layers, Card Dance's and Card Wipe's
  sources and lighting, Shatter's forces and gradients, Ball Action, Pixel Polly and Scatterize,
  Caustics' layers, surface and lighting, Wave World's producers, ground depth, slowing in
  shallow water, damping and height map, and Foam's producer, bubble sizes, lifespans, strength,
  wind, turbulence, flow map, repulsion, pops, wobble and textures. Our own: the stepped
  simulations with kept states (stepper.rs), the sprite and 3D piece renderers (sprites.rs,
  scene3d.rs), Particle Playground's grid each frame and wall reflection, Shatter's puzzle, glass
  and concentric patterns and breaking where a force first touches, Caustics' refraction by
  Snell's law with a surface as high as the layer is wide and light carried down to where it
  lands, Wave World's energy-keeping wave equation, open edges that let the waves out and smooth
  height map, and Foam's clumping by shared movement, squashing wobble, soap-film colours and
  mirror off each ball.
- crates/effects/src/sphere.rs: the immersive family follows EffectCraft's — each effect's
  settings and defaults, the frame layouts, the cube-map face directions and layouts (with names
  of our own for those named after products), the sphere-map and dome mappings, Chromatic
  Aberrations' falloff, Color Gradients' weighting by distance round the sphere, De-Noise's
  guided filter and median, Glow's bright pass, and Digital Glitch's bands, splits, scan lines,
  rate and target. Our own: the blur round whole circles through the poles with fractional box
  widths, more samples where a converted pixel spans many source pixels, the cubic sampling held
  within its nearest pixels, and the fractal noise's evolution between fields kept at strength.
- crates/raster/src/passes.rs, crates/effects/src/passes.rs: the 3D Channel family follows
  EffectCraft's — the channels and names looked for, the effects' settings and defaults, 3D
  Channel Extract's channels, Depth Matte's feather, the focal blur's curve and bias, Fog 3D's
  thickening and gradient layer, ID Matte's coverage, the ID viewer's displays, the channel
  extractor's own-channel names, and the Cryptomatte selection's quoting, patterns and <hex> IDs.
  Cryptomatte itself follows Psyop's published specification and MurmurHash3 Austin Appleby's
  public-domain algorithm. Our own: Depth of Field by ordered depth slices, each carried on under
  nearer ones before blurring, and four points a pixel for jagged edges.
- crates/effects/src/obsolete.rs: the obsolete family follows EffectCraft's — Basic 3D's plane,
  eye and glint, the legacy Gaussian's room to spread, Basic Text's and Path Text's settings
  (shapes, kerning pairs, shear, scale, alignment, margins, visible characters, fade, jitter),
  and Lightning's bolt (wobbling pieces, detail by splitting, branches, pull, core and glow, blend
  modes). Our own: the text in real fonts with fallback, Basic 3D's samples by footprint, and the
  loop spreading its text right round.
- crates/project/src/styles.rs, crates/render/src/styles.rs, crates/engine/src/commands/style.rs:
  layer styles follow EffectCraft's — the styles, their settings, order, defaults and ranges,
  Blending Options and the one Global Light per composition, the drawing order (shadow and glow
  under the layer in their own modes, the inner styles clipped to its shape, then stroke and
  bevel), the shapes from the exact distance transform (Felzenszwalb and Huttenlocher's
  algorithm, already ours in puppet/mesh.rs) and Gaussian blurs, the bevel profiles and their
  lighting, Satin's two copies, Gradient Overlay's styles and the stroke's positions. Our own:
  knockout and the channel switches drawn by the renderer, and styled layers drawn whole.
- crates/project/src/scene.rs, crates/render/src/scene.rs, crates/render/src/three.rs, the 3D parts
  of crates/expr: the 3D renderer follows EffectCraft's — cameras (zoom from the lens length on
  36 mm film, the standard 50 mm camera, two-node aim), the camera, light and material settings
  and defaults, the light falloffs, spot cone and feather, Blinn-Phong shading lit from both
  sides with light transmission and metal, the per-pixel gathering of every plane's piece
  through its inverse homography sorted by depth, shadows ray-cast against the casting planes
  with coloured transmission, and depth of field blurring each layer by its depth. Our own:
  four looks at pixels a crossing goes through, more samples where a plane is seen small, the
  adaptive sunflower sampling of a diffuse light's disc, lighting each layer in its own picture
  before blurring it out of focus (only where it can be seen), motion blur by putting the scene
  together across the shutter, and collapsed 3D compositions joining the outer scene.
- crates/lottie: Lottie export and import follow EffectCraft's lottie crate in what is mapped —
  the document, asset and layer fields, the transform keys, the keyframe ease formulas (speed and
  influence to the normalised `i`/`o` curves and back, `to`/`ti`, `h`), the shape items and their
  settings, mask modes, track mattes, the blend mode order, the text document and animator keys,
  the effects Lottie players draw with their parameter orders and scales, the warnings for what
  Lottie cannot hold, and dotLottie's manifest and folders. The format itself is from the public
  Lottie description (the lottie-spec schema and LottieFiles' documentation). Our own: parents
  and track mattes written by `ind` (both ways), eases worked out before unit changes, layer
  controls by `ind`, every animation of a `.lottie` read (old and new layouts, deflated with our
  own inflate), missing or web pictures kept as missing footage, unreadable pictures named by
  their file, colour alpha folded into still opacity, gaps of 0 written as long as their dash,
  and text as outlines with the letters' true curves.
- crates/zip: deflate moved from the renderer; inflate written from RFC 1951 (Huffman decoding
  by code counts, as Mark Adler's public "puff" describes), zip archives from PKWARE's APPNOTE.
- crates/effects/src/audio.rs, crates/render/src/audio.rs: the audio effects and the mix follow
  EffectCraft's — the thirteen effects with their settings, defaults and ranges, the pre-roll
  that makes blocks sound alike, Audio Levels in dB with −96 as silence, solo and the audio
  switch, compositions mixed at their layer's time, Backwards reading the in–out stretch the
  other way, the peak summary for waveforms. The arithmetic is textbook and public: the RBJ
  "Audio EQ Cookbook" filters, Freeverb's published comb and all-pass lengths, feed-forward
  dynamics with a quadratic soft knee. Our own: time remapped and varying-speed sound read
  sample by sample, levels eased across each block, loudness by ITU-R BS.1770 (K-weighting
  derived for any rate as libebur128 publishes it, gated LUFS), the waveform picture, and
  Convert Audio to Keyframes.
- crates/media: footage probing, numbered sequences and a frame cache follow EffectCraft's media
  crate in what they do; decoding is the system's (crates/os), and the reader kept where it
  stopped, the animated pictures' frame times and the interpretation are our own.
- crates/engine/src/commands/{items,assist,cut,paths,arrange}.rs, crates/path: the project item
  commands, the keyframe assistants (Wiggler, Smoother, Motion Sketch, exponential scale,
  sequence and stagger), the time and layer edits (slip, stretch to, lift and extract the work
  area, crop the composition), the path and mask edits (vertex edits, smart mask interpolation,
  shapes to paths, grouping, nulls from paths, auto-trace) and align and distribute follow
  EffectCraft's commands of those names in what they do. The arithmetic is public: de
  Casteljau subdivision, the Procrustes fit for matching shapes, the least-squares cubic fit
  Philip Schneider gives in Graphics Gems (1990), marching squares and Douglas–Peucker.
- crates/engine/src/commands/{preset,prerender}.rs, crates/media/src/scenes.rs: animation
  presets, pre-render with its post-render actions, proxies and scene edit detection follow
  EffectCraft's command list in what they offer (save and apply a preset; import, import and
  replace usage, set proxy; markers, split, split and precompose). The preset format and the
  presets that come with the program, values worked out on the layer a preset goes on, the
  pre-render lined up in time when only the work area is made, proxies fitted to what they
  stand for, and the cut finder (a brightness grid and colour histograms, a cut being a change
  well above the changes around it) are our own.
- crates/project/src/essential.rs, crates/engine/src/commands/essential.rs: essential properties
  follow EffectCraft's essential graphics in what they hold and do — controls of a composition
  (properties with linked ones, mirrors, media replacement, comments, groups; Source Text as a
  font control, Scale as one value), the essential properties every layer showing it gets, a
  value set on a layer becoming its own (found by what changed), mirrors set on a layer setting
  their control, links following the property that changed, push to source and revert,
  templates. Our own: the properties living in the layer's property tree under essentials/
  so every property command works on them, what a file writes kept until the project is read
  whole, ids kept through merges and duplicates, and the overrides reaching the sound.
- crates/keyframe/src/text.rs, crates/text, crates/render/src/text.rs, crates/engine/src/commands/
  text.rs: the text follows EffectCraft's text in what it offers — character and paragraph styles
  set by range (font, weight, size, tracking, leading, baseline shift, scales, tsume, faux bold
  and italic, caps, super- and subscript, kerning by metrics, optically or by hand, ligatures,
  OpenType features, variable axes; justification of every kind, indents, spacing, direction,
  the every-line and single-line composers, hanging punctuation), vertical text, the animator
  properties and selectors (expression selector, wiggly by dimension), text on a path, fill and
  stroke order, inter-character blending and per-character 3D. Our own: the layout itself (the
  evenest-lines composer, optical kerning from the glyphs' outlines, runs per style and
  direction), the way animators combine, and every implementation.
- crates/text/src/bidi.rs: the Unicode Bidirectional Algorithm written from UAX #9 (the
  character classes and the paired brackets from the Unicode Character Database), checked
  against the system's ICU.
- crates/font/src/shape.rs, crates/font/src/cff.rs: OpenType shaping written from the OpenType
  specification (GSUB, GPOS with variation deltas, GDEF), Arabic joining from the Unicode
  joining types, Hebrew point order from the combining classes, the Type 2 charstrings of CFF
  and CFF2 from Adobe's technical notes 5176 and 5177; checked against CoreText.
- crates/font/src/shape/aat.rs: Apple's layout tables (morx, kerx, kern in Apple's form, ankr,
  trak) written from Apple's TrueType Reference Manual; the feature types and settings are the
  numbers Apple publishes, paired with OpenType's tags as HarfBuzz pairs them (MIT licence; the
  old letter-case small capitals counted as smcp, as HarfBuzz does). Checked against CoreText
  on 55 fonts that come with macOS; where the manual leaves room, CoreText's behaviour is
  followed (a reset across the line keeps later moves, kerx class offsets read in bytes,
  tracking held at the ends of its sizes and given only to fonts with an optical size axis).
  As CoreText does, a font with both Apple's and OpenType's tables is shaped by Apple's, and
  spaced by its kerx where it has one.
- crates/font/src/shape/{indic,khmer,myanmar}.rs: Indic shaping (Devanagari, Bengali, Gurmukhi,
  Gujarati, Oriya, Tamil, Telugu, Kannada, Malayalam), Khmer and Myanmar written from
  Microsoft's script development specifications for OpenType, following HarfBuzz's shapers
  (MIT licence) where the specifications leave room: their syllable grammars, the scripts' reph
  and below-form settings, finding the base by asking the font what it would form, the
  reordering and the cluster merging that goes with it. The characters' categories come from
  the Unicode Character Database (IndicSyllabicCategory and IndicPositionalCategory 13.0, as
  macOS's Perl carries them, and the canonical decompositions), changed as HarfBuzz changes them.
  Checked against CoreText on 500 words in 27 fonts that come with macOS.
- crates/engine/src/commands/{link,group}.rs, the marker commands in time.rs, key.copy, effect.pickColor,
  crates/expr/src/reference.rs: these follow EffectCraft's prop.pickWhip, prop.reset,
  prop.convertExpressionToKeyframes and prop.toggleAnimation in what they do (the pick whip's
  dimension rules; a keyed property reset by a key at the current time). The same holds for the
  property group commands, markers.set, markers.convert, markers.nested,
  layer.updateMarkersFromSource, expr.errors, the Expression Language menu, copying and pasting
  keys, and effect.pickColor (the colour the effect sees, averaged 5 × 5). Our own:
  - each reference checked by working it out, falling back to a plainer one;
  - a reference whose examples the tests run;
  - shape items moved between groups;
  - keys copied across layers by composition time;
  - nested markers placed through time remapping;
  - picked colours brought back from linear light.
- crates/engine/src/commands/{mask,arrange}.rs, crates/path/src/feather.rs, the taper and wave in
  crates/path/src/stroke.rs, the mask settings in crates/project/src/defs.rs and
  crates/render/src/masks.rs: these follow EffectCraft's layer and mask tools in what they do:
  - layer.transform (reset, center, fit, fit width or height, flip) and centre anchor (the
    position's keys moved to make up);
  - per-mask motion blur and feather falloff, and RotoBezier (Catmull-Rom tangents, a sixth of
    the way between a point's neighbours);
  - mask feather points (a signed width and a tension on a piece of the path, travelling with
    its keys; the reach eased along the arc length, round a closed path's start);
  - free transform of points, mask shape and reset;
  - stroke taper (lengths, widths, eases; a quarter-circle ease) and wave (amount, wavelength or
    cycles, phase), with dashes cut along the same length.
  Our own:
  - feather points kept through vertex edits, restarts and reversal;
  - one ramp across both reaches where outward and inward meet (no step at the edge);
  - RotoBezier worked out when drawn rather than stored;
  - wedges filled between the short pieces of a varying stroke;
  - the empty list of softening points kept out of every value.
- crates/engine/src/commands/files.rs, the placeholder footage kind, the colour bars in
  crates/render/src/lib.rs, file times in crates/media/src/pool.rs, and the additions to
  footage.import, footage.reload, footage.replace and project.save follow EffectCraft's file
  commands in what they do. Those commands: missing footage and relink (the other missing files
  found where they were beside it), folder relink (by name, accepted only when the size, kind
  and length match), reload changed footage, placeholders and replace with a placeholder or a
  solid, interpretation applied from other footage, consolidate, reduce, import multiple,
  increment and save, save a copy, item usage, overlay and ripple insert edits. Our own: file
  times kept by the media pool as files are read, missing footage drawn as colour bars, and
  ripple insert cutting layers across the insertion point.
- crates/interchange, crates/export/src/{exr,psd,apart}.rs, crates/engine/src/commands/
  {templates,timeline}.rs, the extra output modules, comp.responsiveTime and Comp::responsive_time:
  these follow EffectCraft's render queue and interchange in what they offer:
  - several output modules, templates, duplicating and moving queue items;
  - frames saved as layered Photoshop files and multi-layer EXR (layer-prefixed channels);
  - composition presets;
  - Responsive Design time (protected regions play at their own speed, the rest takes the
    stretch);
  - timeline import and export in EDL, OpenTimelineIO, Final Cut Pro 7 XML and FCPXML.
  The formats are written and read our own way from their public descriptions: OpenEXR's file
  layout (ZIP blocks of sixteen scanlines with its byte predictor), Adobe's Photoshop file format,
  CMX 3600, the OpenTimelineIO schema, xmeml and FCPXML. Our own:
  - each frame drawn once for every output module;
  - non-overlapping layers packed onto one track while stacking is kept;
  - FCPXML written as one gap with every clip connected in its track's lane;
  - missing timeline media brought in as placeholders.
- crates/engine/src/commands/camera.rs and the Stereo 3D Controls effect in
  crates/effects/src/controls.rs follow EffectCraft's camera tools and rigs (three_d.rs and
  rig3d.rs) in what they do and in these values:
  - an orbit kept within 89° of straight above or below;
  - a pan measured in the picture's pixels where the camera looks (its distance there over its
    zoom);
  - a one-node camera's point taken as far ahead as its zoom;
  - the stereo rig: a controls null with configuration (stereo pair, centre and right, centre and
    left: eyes at −½ and +½, 0 and +1, −1 and 0 scene depths along the camera's right), scene
    depth in % of the composition's width (3 by default), convergence to the point of interest
    or the zoom plus a Z offset; left and right eye compositions holding the source collapsed
    and seen by eye cameras that follow the master by expressions; an output composition
    through 3D Glasses (balanced coloured red–blue by default);
  - the orbit null at the point the camera looks at, the camera's keys moved into its space;
  - a light controlled by a camera: parented at its eye, looking along it, let go where it is.
  Our own:
  - moves worked out in the world and written back in the parent's space;
  - one-node cameras turned with the move (their orientation) rather than reset;
  - look-at fitted exactly to the layers' corners with a margin;
  - focus along the camera's view, linked by expression;
  - the eye cameras' roll following the master;
  - convergence to the focus distance as a third choice.
- crates/effects/src/strokes.rs (the Paint effect), crates/engine/src/commands/paint.rs and
  crates/engine/src/commands/puppet.rs follow EffectCraft's paint tools and puppet recording
  (crates/effects/src/paint.rs and crates/engine/src/commands/{paint,puppet}.rs there) in what
  they do and in these choices:
  - a stroke as dabs of an elliptical tip spaced by a percentage of its diameter;
  - each dab adding its flow (what is left uncovered multiplied by one less the dab's flow),
    the stroke capped at its opacity;
  - the 22 paint modes and the three channel choices (colour into alpha by its luminance);
  - eraser modes for the layer and paint, paint only, or the last stroke only;
  - clone strokes offset by the clone position less the stroke's first point, with a time shift
    or a locked time;
  - the duration choices (constant, write on keying End from 0 to 100 %, single frame, custom);
  - a new Paint effect when the last effect is not Paint;
  - brush tips of hard, soft and flat kinds;
  - a recorded pin drag as a key a frame at a speed, then smoothed;
  - follow-through as an expression adding the leader's delayed move from its rest, cascading
    by distance.
  Our own:
  - strokes timed by from and length, in layer seconds;
  - dab spacing following the tip as pressure shrinks it;
  - clones copying the layer as painted so far;
  - our tip set and names;
  - smooth paths through the points;
  - the recording core shared with Motion Sketch;
  - follow-through falling back to the plainest property reference.
- crates/track, crates/project/src/tracking.rs, crates/effects/src/steady.rs and
  crates/engine/src/commands/track.rs follow EffectCraft's motion tracking (its track crate,
  project tracking layout, Warp Stabilizer effect and track commands) in what they do and in
  these choices:
  - track points of a feature and a search area, with an attach point and offset;
  - normalised cross-correlation searched over a pyramid, the best three places followed down,
    refined by Lucas–Kanade with gain and bias (and optionally the patch's turn and scale);
  - the four low-confidence actions and an 80 % threshold;
  - the tracker kinds (transform, stabilize, parallel and perspective corner pin, raw) and how
    each is applied;
  - a stabilizer analysing frame-to-frame motion by corners followed with pyramidal
    Lucas–Kanade and RANSAC fits for three models, smoothing the camera path with a Gaussian
    average of neighbouring views (after Matsushita et al.) or locking it, then cropping to what
    every frame covers and scaling up within a maximum.
  The methods themselves are textbook: Lewis's fast normalised cross-correlation, Lucas–Kanade
  and Baker–Matthews, Bouguet's pyramidal Lucas–Kanade, Shi–Tomasi corners, Fischler–Bolles
  RANSAC, Hartley's normalised DLT. Our own:
  - the code throughout;
  - the forward-backward check on followed corners;
  - the camera path mirrored past the clip's ends;
  - Rolling Shutter Repair shifting each row by the frame's motion over its share of the read
    time;
  - a mask tracker re-finding corners inside the moved outline each frame;
  - the analysis kept as text in the effect, so settings change without analysing again.
- crates/model, crates/render/src/meshes.rs, the 3D model layer and
  crates/engine/src/commands/model.rs follow EffectCraft's 3D models (its model crate, Advanced
  3D renderer and model commands) in what they do and in these choices:
  - glTF 2.0 and OBJ/MTL read into meshes of primitives with metallic-roughness materials, a
    node tree, cameras, lights, skins and animations;
  - OBJ shininess taken as roughness;
  - flat normals for primitives without normals, and tangents made for normal maps;
  - cameras and lights from a model made layers: a glTF camera's vertical field of view as zoom,
    a light's unit as 100 % intensity, a spot's outer cone doubled as the cone angle and the
    inner one as feather.
  The formats are read from their public descriptions: the Khronos glTF 2.0 specification,
  KHR_lights_punctual, KHR_materials_unlit, KHR_materials_emissive_strength, and the Wavefront
  OBJ and MTL descriptions. Our own:
  - the readers, the rasterizer (four samples a pixel, near-plane clipping, perspective-correct
    shading per triangle per pixel, bands of rows in parallel);
  - models joining the per-pixel depth sorting of flat 3D layers, receiving their shadows;
  - the metallic-roughness material mapped onto layer material options;
  - the default key and fill light where a composition has none;
  - a unit as `unitScale` pixels, sized at first to fill half the composition's height, centred on
    the model's box;
  - pictures inside model files decoded through ImageIO from memory.
- crates/render/src/extrude.rs, crates/path/src/caps.rs and the Geometry Options group follow
  EffectCraft's extruded text and shape layers (its model crate's extrude and triangulate, and
  the Geometry Options of its Advanced 3D renderer) in what they offer: bevel style (none,
  angular, concave, convex), bevel depth, hole bevel depth and extrusion depth, caps cut into
  triangles by ear clipping with holes bridged in. Our own:
  - the solid made from the outlines traced from the layer's own picture (so fills, strokes,
    gradients and effects all show on its faces);
  - inset bevel rings untangled where they cross themselves, kept one to one with their
    neighbours;
  - sides coloured as the picture's edges;
  - drawn, lit, shadowed and defocused as 3D models are.
- crates/model/src/shapes.rs, crates/model/src/stl.rs, the 3D primitive layers, layer.newPrimitive
  and material.set, material.reset and material.duplicateAssign follow EffectCraft's parametric
  primitives (its model crate's prim and stl, and its model3d commands) in what they offer and in
  these values: the six solids and how each is laid out (faces, poles, seams, caps, texture
  points), the default sizes (300 px, a 400 px plane, radius 150, tube 50, 48 segments, 24 rings),
  a plane seen from both sides, a primitive's base colour, metallic, roughness and emissive in its
  Material Options, and copying a material onto layers that share its options. STL is read from
  3D Systems' description of the format and its Magics and VisCAM colour extensions, turned from
  Z-up to Y-up as theirs is. Our own: the solids made in the layer's own space and drawn by our
  model renderer, kept between frames, and the commands built on layer.add and prop.set.
- crates/psd and footage.import's `as` (a Photoshop document as a composition) follow
  EffectCraft's Photoshop import (its psd crate and psd_import) in what they do and in these
  choices: each pixel layer footage of that one layer (the document's size, or retaining its
  own), groups as compositions with pass-through groups collapsed, blend mode keys mapped as
  theirs are, opacity times fill opacity, hidden layers off, clipped layers preserving the
  transparency under them; and the file read from Adobe's published Photoshop File Formats
  Specification. Our own: the reader, its decompression (inflate from our zip crate), the
  colour conversions, the small writer for test documents, and adjustment and fill layers left
  out and listed rather than drawn from their stand-in pixels.
- crates/svg and layer.shapesFromVector follow EffectCraft's SVG import (its svg crate) and
  Create Shapes from Vector Layer in what they read and do: the elements, properties, units,
  colour forms, gradients, `use`/`symbol`, nested viewports, style sheets with simple selectors
  and their strength, and these choices: the 300 × 150 default size, a size kept in proportion
  when only one is given, gradient stops never earlier than the last, a lone stop as a colour,
  a focal point kept inside its circle, at most 500,000 elements made by `use`, text, pictures,
  filters, clipping and masks passed over and listed. Written from the W3C SVG 1.1 Second Edition
  and SVG 2 recommendations and CSS Color Level 3 (its named colours). Our own: the XML reader,
  the drawing (our path filler and stroker, gradients pixel by pixel, groups that fade drawn on
  their own), the picture drawn at the detail it is seen with, and the shape items it becomes.
- crates/effects/src/blur.rs Zoom Blur and Camera-Shake Deblur follow EffectCraft's CC Radial Blur
  and Camera-Shake Deblur (its effects crate's blur3) in what they do and in these values: the
  five kinds and how each streak is sampled and weighed (a zoom as half the amount's part of the
  distance, a turn as a quarter of its degrees, samples by Quality); the deblur's patches judged
  by their mean squared brightness steps against the frames within Blur Duration, a sharper
  neighbour's patch lined up by the least squared differences within the search range (half of
  it for Standard) and blended between patch middles, Shake Sensitivity and Threshold setting how
  much sharper it must be, Strength pushing past it. Our own: the code, reading neighbouring
  frames through our effect host.
- The environment light (an environment light kind with a source layer and rotation, the
  environment layer and the environment background layer, layer.environment and
  light.environmentBackground) follows EffectCraft's Environment light, Environment Layer and
  Create Environment Light Background Layer in what they do and in these choices: the panorama
  laid out as an equirectangular picture with up at its top (its u and v from a direction as
  theirs are), turned about the vertical by the light's rotation; the picture halved down to 8
  pixels for rougher shine, at most 1024 across; the light falling on a surface from a 32 × 16
  map weighed by the cosine and the sphere's area; the split-sum shine with Karis's analytic fit
  (from "Real Shading in Unreal Engine 4", 2013); the backdrop seen through the camera as if
  endlessly far. Our own: the environment's light added to our flat 3D layers' lighting (their
  diffuse and specular options at 50 % as it is, roughness from their shininess) and to our
  models', and the panorama taken as the source layer's own rectangle.
- crates/exr (reading) and EXR footage's passes follow EffectCraft's multi-layer OpenEXR channels
  (its media crate's exr_channels) in what they do and in these choices: every channel of every
  part a plane named `layer.channel` (the part's name, unless the channel already carries it, as
  Blender 5 writes them), placed in the display window with nothing outside a part's data
  window (the background's far depth for a depth), Cryptomatte manifests from the
  `cryptomatte/<key>/name` and `…/manifest` attributes, and the picture of a file of many layers:
  its unnamed colour layer, else the beauty pass by its name (combined, beauty, image, rgba, rgb,
  color, colour), else the first colour layer that is not a data pass, else a first channel as
  grey. Written from the OpenEXR file layout and technical introduction (Academy Software
  Foundation, BSD-3-Clause) and its codecs as OpenEXR has them — PIZ's Huffman codes and
  wavelet, PXR24, B44's blocks and its exp/log tables, DWA's channel rules, DCT, colour transform
  and perceptual curve, and the luminance/chroma conversion — checked against files OpenEXR
  writes. The reference read files with a third-party crate; ours is our own: the reader, every
  decompressor (inflate from our zip crate), the threads it decodes on, the luminance/chroma
  weights from the file's primaries, the colours made sRGB as the system makes other pictures
  (premultiplied in linear light by default, as the format has them), and Blender's names for
  its passes. The test files in crates/exr/fixtures were written by OpenEXR 3.4 through Blender
  5.2 (its OpenImageIO, and a render of a small scene), with the scripts beside them.
- A composition's render passes (render/src/passes.rs, for 3D Channel effects on a layer showing
  it) follow EffectCraft's comp auxiliary pass (its render's three_d compose aux_pass) in what
  they hold and in these choices: Z from the camera, ObjectID the layer's number in the
  composition, MaterialID its source item's, Coverage, normals in the camera's space turned to
  face it, UV across the layer, Cryptomatte by layer names and by source names with four ranks
  each and their manifests; 2D layers lying flat at the depth of the composition's middle;
  each pixel's pieces taken from the front, its values the first covering at least half (else
  the one that shows most), ranks by coverage then ID. Our own: the pieces recorded by our 3D
  renderer as it lays out its scene (so extruded text and shapes, primitives and models take
  part, with their own normals and texture points, and a model's materials by their names), and
  a layer inside a collapsed composition numbered by the layer it is in.
